      <div type="commentary">
        <p xml:lang="en">The donor names on the narrow sides identify five individuals; one bearer
          of the surname Yang 羊 is possibly connected to Yang Lie 羊烈, governor of Yangping
          陽平 (558–561) — see the upstream discussion references.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="translation" xml:lang="en"><p>
                    Sutra Donors, Monk (<hi rend="italic"><foreign xml:lang="sa">bhikṣu</foreign></hi>) […][…] and  […][…] from Baishi Monastery;
                    the Reclusive [...],<note>His title may have been Reclusive Gentleman (<hi rend="italic">xianlang </hi><foreign xml:lang="zh">仙郞</foreign>).</note> Gao Wan­tai from […][…];<note>The two missing characters may have been a place name.</note>
                    Fasong from Shiku Monastery.
                    The association member, Yang Mu, Recorder of Yan­zhou; the association member Yang Shizi, son of <foreign xml:lang="sa">Śākya</foreign>.</p><p>Sutra Donor Yang Zhong from Mount Tai, General Formidable and Powerful,<note>This title is mentioned several times in the <hi rend="italic">History of the Northern Dynasties</hi> (<hi rend="italic">Beishi </hi><foreign xml:lang="zh">北史</foreign>).</note> Governor of East Yang­ping, Yan­zhou, and Governor of the upper Commandery of Wucheng in Yizhou.<note>Yizhou was established under the Northern Wei dynasty. The seat of this prefecture was located in today’s Weihui <foreign xml:lang="zh">衛輝</foreign> in Henan Province, about 240 km to the west of Mount Shuiniu. According to <ref type="biblio" target="Weishu1974">7: 2486–2487</ref>, it was subdivided into seven commanderies and nineteen counties. The Commandery of Wucheng was established in 547, the fifth year of the <hi rend="italic">wuding</hi> era of the Eastern Wei dynasty.</note>
                    Shu Shigui of the Commandery Personal Evaluation Section.
                    The association member, Audience Attendant,<note>According to <ref type="biblio" target="Hucker1985">212</ref>, an “official designation of numerous low-ranking members of the Department of Scholarly Counselors.”</note> Yang Shan.
                    The association member Yang Wansui. </p><p>The Superintendent of Monks,<note>The Superintendent of Monks (<hi rend="italic">shamen du</hi><foreign xml:lang="zh">沙門都</foreign>) is subordinated to General Superintendent of Monks (<hi rend="italic">shamen tong</hi><foreign xml:lang="zh">沙門統</foreign>). Both are supervised by the Great General Superintendent of Monks (<hi rend="italic">da shamen tong</hi><foreign xml:lang="zh">大沙門統</foreign>), the highest administrative position in the state. For more details see <ref type="biblio" target="XieChongguang2009">51–86</ref>.</note> Mingjian, Monk (<hi rend="italic"><foreign xml:lang="sa">bhikṣu</foreign></hi>) […][…], Monk (<hi rend="italic"><foreign xml:lang="sa">bhikṣu</foreign></hi>) Seng[…], Monk (<hi rend="italic"><foreign xml:lang="sa">bhikṣu</foreign></hi>) […]liao, and Monk (<hi rend="italic"><foreign xml:lang="sa">bhikṣu</foreign></hi>) […][…] from Baishi Monastery.
                    […][…][…][…][…] Song Xu[…]. Commandery Rectifier, Shu Hujiang. […][…][…][…][…][…][…][…][…][…] Shu Yuan[…] ...</p><p>Tanchao of Longhua Monastery. […][…] from Shu Lingzhen Monastery. Great […][…][…][… ][…][…][…][…]. […][…] […][…] […][…] from Zhonghe Monastery. Commandery Rectifier, Shu Yuan. Shu […][…] of the Commandery Personal Evaluation Section. </p><p>Great Chief Overseer, Sang Mingda. Great Chief Overseer, Shu […][…][…][…][…][…][…][…][…][…]. Great Chief Overseer, […][…] ...</p></div>
